course: dictionary
lesson: 5298
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and context tells you which one the writer wants. The word justice is a good example. Justice can mean fairness, the system of laws and courts, or a judge. Read the words around it and ask which meaning fits.
explain: «The thief was brought to justice.» Here justice means the system of laws and courts that deals with crimes. Brought to justice means caught and punished. Check by swapping in your meaning: «The thief was caught and punished» still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: «Restorative justice brings the wrongdoer and the person harmed together to put things right.» In this sentence justice means fair treatment that puts right the harm done. «This photo doesn't do you justice» means the photo shows you less well than you really look, so do justice means show something as well as it deserves.
fact: Justice means fairness: treating people in a fair and right way.
fact: Justice can also mean the system of laws, courts and punishments a country uses to deal with crimes.
fact: A justice is a judge, especially in a high court; a Justice of the Peace is a local judge.
fact: «Brought to justice» means caught and punished.
fact: «Do justice to» means show something as well as it deserves.
exercise: «The villain was finally brought to justice.» What does brought to justice mean? | Given a prize for being good | Asked to say sorry only | *Caught and punished | Sent to bed early
exercise: «Restorative justice brings the wrongdoer and the person harmed together to put things right.» What does this suggest justice does here? | Ends all talking | *Helps people put right the harm done | Makes the wrongdoer rich | Gives out prizes
exercise: Which word means a judge, as in a Justice of the Peace? | Witness | Jury | Victim | *Justice
test: «Peace talks about land, safety and justice can take years.» Which meaning of justice fits best? | A sort of weather | A loud noise | *Fair and right treatment for everyone | A type of bird
test: Which word could replace justice in «a Justice of the Peace»? | Cook | Fireman | *Judge | Shopkeeper
exam: «This photo doesn't do you justice» means the photo shows you | *less well than you really look | with no colour | very slowly | better than a painting
